conhost: pasting types the terminating NUL as a character.

`paste_clipboard` turns clipboard text into key events, and sizes the loop with

    unsigned int i, len = GlobalSize(h) / sizeof(WCHAR);

`GlobalSize` reports the size of the *allocation*, not the length of the string.
A `CF_UNICODETEXT` handle is NUL-terminated by contract, and conhost's own copy
path writes that terminator deliberately -- `copy_selection` sets
`*end = '\0'` on the last row and then `GlobalReAlloc`s to a size that includes
it. So the block always holds at least one WCHAR of NUL past the text, plus
whatever the allocator rounded up to.

The loop then faithfully types all of it. Pasting `abc` sends a, b, c and then a
literal U+0000 as `uChar.UnicodeChar`, which the console renders as a box,
because a box is what a font draws for a character with no glyph.

Reported on the device pasting an API token out of Android's clipboard into
PowerShell: the token itself arrived intact and was followed by boxes. That is
the shape this predicts -- the text is never corrupted, it is only ever followed
by however many NULs the allocation carried.

**Truncating at the first NUL cannot lose real text**, which is the reason this
is a two-line fix rather than a heuristic. `CF_UNICODETEXT` is NUL-terminated,
so the first NUL *is* the end of the string; anything after it is padding by
definition. Nothing that is legitimately on a text clipboard contains an embedded
NUL.

The same expression appears nowhere else in conhost. The other `GlobalSize`
callers in the tree are in `dlls/ole32/clipboard.c`, which moves arbitrary
structured-storage payloads rather than text -- truncating those at a zero byte
would be actively wrong, so they are left alone.

diff --git a/programs/conhost/window.c b/programs/conhost/window.c
index f8fc7016af1..2784d4ef6fb 100644
--- a/programs/conhost/window.c
+++ b/programs/conhost/window.c
@@ -1060,12 +1060,28 @@ static void paste_clipboard( struct console *console )
     if (h && (ptr = GlobalLock( h )))
     {
         unsigned int i, len = GlobalSize(h) / sizeof(WCHAR);
         INPUT_RECORD ir[2];
         SHORT sh;

+        /* Vessel: the string, not the allocation.
+         *
+         * GlobalSize reports the size of the block, which holds the
+         * terminating NUL and whatever the allocator rounded up to. The loop
+         * below turns every WCHAR in that range into a key event, so pasting
+         * `abc` types a, b, c and then a literal U+0000 -- which the console
+         * draws as a box, because that is what a font does with a character
+         * that has no glyph. Reported on the device pasting an API token from
+         * Android's clipboard: the token arrived correct and was followed by
+         * boxes.
+         *
+         * CF_UNICODETEXT is NUL-terminated by contract, so the first NUL is
+         * the end of the text and everything after it is padding. */
+        for (i = 0; i < len; i++) if (!ptr[i]) break;
+        len = i;
+
         ir[0].EventType = KEY_EVENT;
         ir[0].Event.KeyEvent.wRepeatCount = 0;
         ir[0].Event.KeyEvent.dwControlKeyState = 0;
         ir[0].Event.KeyEvent.bKeyDown = TRUE;

         /* generate the corresponding input records */
